{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1546801117,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MTc2NTQwMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3105#issuecomment-451765401"},"message":"Interesting find/analysis... Thanks, yet to read it in full and provide feedback","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1546801310,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MTc2NTYxOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3105#issuecomment-451765619"},"message":"FTR: Here are the bits that introduced this choice (with some stats): https://github.com/datalad/datalad/pull/1630","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1546876375,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MTk4MDM3Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3105#issuecomment-451980372"},"message":"It looks as if a sensible alternative would be to keep lzma compression (same storage impact), but replace the xz container format with standard ZIP. ZIP also supports LZMA and the Python zipfile module supports it since Python 3.3.\n\nI could not find a backport of zipfile for Python 2, but issue will resolve itself at the end of the year.\n\nhttps://docs.python.org/3.5/library/zipfile.html#zipfile.ZIP_LZMA\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zip_(file_format)#Compression_methods\n\nAn alternative could be https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lzip but I did not see Python support for it.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1546876375,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE2ODgzMjU4OA=="},"target":"7276aa40627a5aa05e47fe34b5026d04b830f6a6870df363df3de06eb498b2ab","message":"It looks as if a sensible alternative would be to keep lzma compression (same storage impact), but replace the xz container format with standard ZIP. ZIP also supports LZMA and the Python zipfile module supports it since Python 3.3.\n\nI could not find a backport of zipfile for Python 2, but this issue would resolve itself at the end of the year.\n\nhttps://docs.python.org/3.5/library/zipfile.html#zipfile.ZIP_LZMA\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zip_(file_format)#Compression_methods\n\nAn alternative could be https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lzip but I did not see Python support for it.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1546876523,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE2ODgzMzc1Mg=="},"target":"7276aa40627a5aa05e47fe34b5026d04b830f6a6870df363df3de06eb498b2ab","message":"It looks as if a sensible alternative would be to keep lzma compression (same storage impact), but replace the xz container format with standard ZIP. ZIP also supports LZMA and the Python zipfile module supports it since Python 3.3.\n\nI could not find a backport of zipfile for Python 2, but this issue would resolve itself at the end of the year.\n\nhttps://docs.python.org/3.5/library/zipfile.html#zipfile.ZIP_LZMA\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zip_(file_format)#Compression_methods\n\nAn alternative could be https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lzip but I did not see Python support for it.\n\nOne has to appreciate the author's attitude:\n\n\u003e The lzip format is as simple as possible (but not simpler). The lzip manual provides the source code of a simple decompressor along with a detailed explanation of how it works, so that with the only help of the lzip manual it would be possible for a digital archaeologist to extract the data from a lzip file long after quantum computers eventually render LZMA obsolete.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1546876547,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE2ODgzMzg3OA=="},"target":"7276aa40627a5aa05e47fe34b5026d04b830f6a6870df363df3de06eb498b2ab","message":"It looks as if a sensible alternative would be to keep lzma compression (same storage impact), but replace the xz container format with standard ZIP. ZIP also supports LZMA and the Python zipfile module supports it since Python 3.3.\n\nI could not find a backport of zipfile for Python 2, but this issue would resolve itself at the end of the year.\n\nhttps://docs.python.org/3.5/library/zipfile.html#zipfile.ZIP_LZMA\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zip_(file_format)#Compression_methods\n\nAn alternative could be https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lzip but I did not see Python support for it.\n\nOne has to appreciate the author's attitude:\n\n\u003e The lzip format is as simple as possible (but not simpler). The lzip manual provides the source code of a simple decompressor along with a detailed explanation of how it works, so that with the only help of the lzip manual it would be possible for a digital archaeologist to extract the data from a lzip file long after quantum computers eventually render LZMA obsolete.\n\nhttp://lzip.nongnu.org/lzip.html","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1546878853,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MTk4MDU2Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3105#issuecomment-451980567"},"message":"Here is what we would get now for all our extracted metadata at datasets.datalad.org placed into a .tar (1.074GB) with default and `-9` for gzip, bzip2 (-9 produced the same as default) and xz:\n```shell\n$\u003e du -scm objects*tar*\n1074    objects.tar\n166     objects-default.tar.gz\n161     objects-9.tar.gz\n103     objects.tar.bz2\n38      objects-default.tar.xz\n36      objects-9.tar.xz\n```","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1546878853,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE2ODg1MDc0OQ=="},"target":"95676738f19944de29c2f060b775974101cbae9b8baeb91b11e14515b48c72e4","message":"Here is what we would get now for all our extracted metadata at datasets.datalad.org placed into a .tar (1.074GB) with default and `-9` for gzip, bzip2 (-9 produced the same as default), lzip and xz:\n```shell\n$\u003e du -scm objects*tar*\n1074    objects.tar\n166     objects-default.tar.gz\n161     objects-9.tar.gz\n103     objects.tar.bz2\n39      objects-default.tar.lz\n38      objects-default.tar.xz\n36      objects-9.tar.xz\n35      objects-9.tar.lz\n```","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1546878888,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MTk5NTM0NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3105#issuecomment-451995345"},"message":"note: Added bzip2 and lzip to the report [above](https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3105#issuecomment-451980567), lzip -9 comes up as a winner","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1546895881,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MjA4NTA5Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3105#issuecomment-452085093"},"message":"here is some discussion on the analysis on https://www.reddit.com/r/linux/comments/58swzc/xz_format_inadequate_for_longterm_archiving/\nwhich seems to summarize for mortals/us that unless file is damaged we should be just fine.  And we have git/annex to validate transmission etc. To that degree the widespread of .xz use in the wild (debian, gnome, etc) attests - so far I've not heard about \"oh, .xz killed my data\".","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1546924433,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MjE3ODAzNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3105#issuecomment-452178035"},"message":"Hmm, I remember \"do not use xz for this and that debian package\" and the lzip package also has a discussion on failure to extract on busybox.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1546940848,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MjIzNzE3Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3105#issuecomment-452237172"},"message":"\u003e which seems to summarize for mortals/us that unless file is damaged we should be just fine. And we have git/annex to validate transmission \n\nI don't think this is correct. It's not just about transmission errors. The flaws regarding error detection/correction are concerned with the comparison of the compressed, containerized data within the xz file to the original, uncompressed data. Annex doesn't know anything about the latter. If you added a corrupted xz file, that's fine for annex (of course!). And generally that's okay for us as well. But: If you look in that analysis (unchecked length fields for example), it might well be, that the corruption remains undiscovered during decompression. And that is a problem. You might notice only by a human looking at the actual decompressed data. That's bad design indeed. If there's a better way, particularly while still using the same compression algorithm, we should go there, I think.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1546941237,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MjIzOTE1Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3105#issuecomment-452239153"},"message":"Note, that this is different from using it purely for delivering. If you have an uncompressed file and a checksum for it, then compress and deliver it, decompress and compare the checksum for the extracted file again - then you have the means to detect the corruption and don't need another one provided by the compression container. But this is not our situation.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1546966036,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MjM2ODc3OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3105#issuecomment-452368779"},"message":"\u003e Hmm, I remember \"do not use xz for this and that debian package\" and the lzip package also has a discussion on failure to extract on busybox.\n\nI only remember \"do not use .xz for source packages since older dpkg we use in neurodebian's backports, doesn't support it\".  do you have an example url?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1546966162,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MjM2OTYwMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3105#issuecomment-452369601"},"message":"\u003e But: If you look in that analysis (unchecked length fields for example), it might well be, that the corruption remains undiscovered during decompression. And that is a problem. You might notice only by a human looking at the actual decompressed data.\n\nsounds like there should then be an example file/invocation which leads to such a corrupted file.  Could you point to one?","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1549538451,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDIxMjM3NjgzNzc="},"added":["metadata"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1552751354,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3MzU1MTQ1Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3105#issuecomment-473551453"},"message":"Buzz @bpoldrack and @mih if you are aware of the specific example cases where .xz performs incorrectly beyond inability to catch transmission errors? If not, then IMHO there is no rush to switch, although switching probably would be warranted whenever there is a ready to use alternative implementation.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1552752497,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3MzU1NjcwOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3105#issuecomment-473556708"},"message":"I could not find any concrete case that is immediately relevant for us. We could close this, and I will now.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1552752497,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjIwODI0NTA3Mg=="},"status":2}]}